{"id":"10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2024.108056","venue":"j-8ec1c3871b","year":2024,"track":null,"title":"Feature engineered embeddings for classification of molecular data","authors":["Claudio Jardim","Alta de Waal","Inger Fabris-Rotelli","Najmeh Nakhaei Rad","Jocelyn Mazarura","Dean Sherry"],"author_ids":["https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0429-1784","https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2582-0868"],"abstract":"The classification of molecules is of particular importance to the drug discovery process and several other use cases. Data in this domain can be partitioned into structural and sequence/text data. Several techniques such as deep learning are able to classify molecules and predict their functions using both types of data. Molecular structure and encoded chemical information are sufficient to classify a characteristic of a molecule. However, the use of a molecule's structural information typically requires large amounts of computational power with deep learning models that take a long time to train. In this study, we present an alternative approach to molecule classification that addresses the limitations of other techniques. This approach uses natural language processing techniques in the form of count vectorisation, term frequency-inverse document frequency, word2vec and Latent Dirichlet Allocation to feature engineer molecular text data. Through this approach, we aim to make a robust and easily reproducible embedding that is fast to implement and solely dependent on chemical (text) data such as the sequence of a protein. Further, we investigate the usefulness of these embeddings for machine learning models. We apply the techniques to two different types of molecular text data: FASTA sequence data and Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry Specification data. We show that these embeddings provide excellent performance for classification.","keywords":[],"tldr":null,"primary_area":null,"presentation":null,"doi":"10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2024.108056","pages":"108056","bibtex":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2024.108056","pdf_url":null,"code_url":null,"project_url":null,"published":"2024-06","source":"crossref.org+openalex.org","venue_type":"journal","journal_title":"Computational Biology and Chemistry","issn":"1476-9271","article_type":"unknown","screening_status":"needs_article_type","conference_title":null,"ccf_rank":null,"ccf_field":null,"ccf_catalog_version":null,"ccf_paper_status":null,"dblp_key":null,"dblp_toc":null,"booktitle":null,"publisher_url":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1476927124000446","ai4s_hits":["\\bmolecules?\\b","\\bmolecular\\b","drug discovery","\\bproteins?\\b"],"ai4s_reason":"keyword","ai4s_tags":["AI for 生命科学","AI for 物质科学"],"ai4s_keywords":["protein","drug discovery","molecule"],"ai4s_tagging":{"taxonomy_version":"1.2","basis":"metadata_rules","status":"needs_review","evidence":{"AI for 生命科学":["protein","drug discovery"],"AI for 物质科学":["molecule"]},"method_evidence":[]}},
{"id":"10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2024.108069","venue":"j-8ec1c3871b","year":2024,"track":null,"title":"Accuracy of AlphaFold models: Comparison with short N O contacts in atomic resolution protein crystal structures","authors":["Oliviero Carugo"],"author_ids":["https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2924-9016"],"abstract":"Artificial intelligence (AI) has revolutionized structural biology by predicting protein 3D structures with near-experimental accuracy. Here, short backbone N-O distances in high-resolution crystal structures were compared to those in three-dimensional models based on AI AlphaFold/ColabFold, specifically considering their estimated standard errors. Experimental and computationally modeled distances very often differ significantly, showing that these models' precision is inadequate to reproduce experimental results at high resolution. T-tests and normal probability plots showed that these computational methods predict atomic position standard errors 3.5-6 times bigger than experimental errors. SYNOPSIS: Positional standard errors in AI-based protein 3D models are 3.5-6 times larger than in atomic resolution crystal structures.","keywords":[],"tldr":null,"primary_area":null,"presentation":null,"doi":"10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2024.108069","pages":"108069","bibtex":null,"url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2024.108069","pdf_url":null,"code_url":null,"project_url":null,"published":"2024-06","source":"crossref.org+openalex.org","venue_type":"journal","journal_title":"Computational Biology and Chemistry","issn":"1476-9271","article_type":"unknown","screening_status":"needs_article_type","conference_title":null,"ccf_rank":null,"ccf_field":null,"ccf_catalog_version":null,"ccf_paper_status":null,"dblp_key":null,"dblp_toc":null,"booktitle":null,"publisher_url":"https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1476927124000574","ai4s_hits":["crystal structure","alphafold","\\bproteins?\\b"],"ai4s_reason":"keyword","ai4s_tags":["AI for 生命科学","AI for 物质科学"],"ai4s_keywords":["protein","crystal"],"ai4s_tagging":{"taxonomy_version":"1.2","basis":"metadata_rules","status":"needs_review","evidence":{"AI for 生命科学":["protein"],"AI for 物质科学":["crystal"]},"method_evidence":[]}},
